  "decision": {
    "answer": "SUPPORTS",
    "explanation": "The evidence from pmid-26989027 explicitly identifies KRAS Q61L mutations in non-responding metastatic colorectal cancer (mCRC) patients treated with cetuximab. Specifically, the text states that among non-responders, two Q61L (exon 3) KRAS mutations were detected. This directly supports the claim that KRAS Q61L is a predictive biomarker for resistance to cetuximab in colorectal cancer.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "document_id": "pmid-26989027",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 475,
        "quote": "A total of 53 mCRC patients were treated with cetuximab in combination with chemotherapy either as a first line (n = 39, 73.5%) or third line (n = 14, 26.4%) therapy . . . Among the 23 non-responders, 4 (17.4%) subjects had a non-exon 2 KRAS mutation, 3 (13%) subjects had a BRAF mutation, and 2 subjects (8.7%) had an NRAS mutation (Figure 2B) . . . For example, two Q61L (exon 3), one Q61H (exon 3), and one A146T (exon 4) KRAS mutations were detected in the non-responders"
      }
    ],
